So make sure you're subscribed to the newsletter.

283
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
It comes by email once a month

284
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And to subscribe you can go to scoutmastertypepadcom

285
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
and there you go, You'll be all set.

286
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
Well, we're going to talk a little bit about

287
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
welcoming Weeblows into the troop

288
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

289
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
All right, we got a couple other things to do

290
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
and that's plenty for this podcast.

291
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
So let's get started, shall we

292
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less?

293
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Well, it's that time of year,

294
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
It's time to welcome new Scouts

295
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:29,000
who are crossing the bridge from their Weeblows, Dad.

296
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
And while I'm not going to go into

297
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
step-by-step logistics here,

298
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
I think it's important for us to take a moment

299
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and discuss some of the broad underlying concepts

300
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
that are going to help make these new Scouts

301
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
into old Scouts one day.

302
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Conventional wisdom says that that first year

303
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
of a Scout's experience in his new troop

304
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:51,000
is very, very important

305
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:56,000
And it has a large bearing on his continuing in Scouting

306
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
for the next several years.

307
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Now there's a lot of shifting alliances

308
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
and interests in adolescence

309
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
that are beyond the control of any Scout Master.

310
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
But there are some important steps

311
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
that we can take to assure that

312
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
we're going to get these new guys

313
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:13,000
and they are going to stick with the program

314
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
and they're going to get something out of it.

315
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
Perhaps the first one is to kind of analyze

316
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
the relationships that are going on.

317
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:23,000
There's two or three different things to look at.

318
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
One is a relationship between your older Scouts

319
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
and your younger Scouts.

320
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
The other one is a relationship

321
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
between the newer Scouts

322
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
who are coming into the troop

323
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,000
that they have with each other,

324
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
And one is between your new family's parents

325
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
and yourself and the other adults

326
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
who are involved with the troop.

327
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
I wouldn't assume, because we below has joined a troop

328
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
as a den, that they should all remain together as a group.

329
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Now. they may have been great high functioning,

330
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
They may have given great chemistry

331
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
amongst those boys and they may be good pals.

332
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
And you know, after the past couple of years

333
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
of being in a we below's den together,

334
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
maybe a couple of them have worn out

335
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,000
their welcome with each other.

336
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
So talking to a den leader

337
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:10,000
or the den chief who you've had in with,

338
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
that we below's den is a good idea.

339
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Which of these boys work well together,

340
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
which of them are kind of bad juju,

341
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
if you know what I mean.

342
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
Which of them kind of set each other off?

343
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
That might be a big thing for their first year.

344
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
in making a few adjustments.

345
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
Many troops use the new scout patrol plan

346
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
very successfully.

347
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
I've adapted some of the concepts

348
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
and made a couple of changes

349
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:37,000
to adapt it to work in our troop.

350
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
A lot of times a we below's den will form a new patrol

351
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and sometimes they'll become members of an existing patrol.

352
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:47,000
It really depends on the boys who are coming in

353
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:51,000
and the way that our patrols are set up at the time.

354
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
What I think is important is the scouts

355
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
aren't in some kind of junior patrol

356
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
or in a situation where they're

357
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
just totally overseen by adults

358
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
or have a lot of adult involvement in their patrol,

359
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
because that's basically just like

360
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
doing a third year of we below's.

361
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Now I think they need to be a good, strong,

362
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
independent patrol, off on their own

363
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
doing their stuff.

364
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:15,000
It will take them a little while to catch on to that,

365
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:18,000
but a little mentoring and a little oversight

366
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:22,000
is a good thing, but giving them a lot of room

367
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:26,000
and making sure that they are in a real patrol.

368
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Let's talk about the relationship

369
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:32,000
between your new scouts and your older scouts.

370
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
This can be a problem sometimes.

371
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
The attitude with which your troop

372
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
receives new scouts is crucial.

373
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
Now I don't have to really tell anybody

374
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
listening to this that we don't do hazing

375
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
or initiations or bullying in scouts.

376
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
We don't do that for adults,

377
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
We don't do that for youth.

378
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
Anyone who walks through the door

379
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and signs a form is a full member

380
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
of scouting and your scout troop.

381
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
They're a full member of their patrol.

382
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:02,000
They are full participants

383
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
in every aspect of the program

384
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
and the program is planned to accommodate them

385
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
as well as the older scouts.

386
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Now, it might take a little explaining,

387
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:13,000
It might take a little time,

388
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
but older scouts need to understand

389
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
that leadership is an opportunity for service,

390
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,000
not an excuse for exercising

391
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
their lordly authority over the little guys.

392
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:29,000
It's also important to them in their scouting

393
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:30,000
to advance in rank.

394
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:31,000
They're going to need to have

395
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
a position of responsibility,

396
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and who are they going to be responsible for?

397
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Well, these new guys.

398
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
Now, the new guys are going to tire them out.

399
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
The new guys aren't going to know what to do.

400
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
The new guys aren't going to be all that cool,

401
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
but we're going to work along with our older boys

402
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
to make sure that they understand

403
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
the importance of new scouts in their troop,

404
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
to remind them to be a little empathetic,

405
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
to remember what their experience was

406
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,000
when they came into the troop and how they felt

407
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and the way that they looked up to

408
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:07,000
or respected the older scouts

409
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
or maybe had a problem with them.

410
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:13,000
But a little discussion about this is always in order

411
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
and it's always a good thing to sit down

412
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
with your youth leadership

413
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
and remind them how important all of this is

414
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
and how important it is to be friendly and kind

415
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:26,000
to the new scouts who are coming into the troop.

416
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
We want to have lots of positives.

417
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
We want to have acceptance and support

418
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and positive reinforcement for our new guys.

419
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,000
We need to take every opportunity

420
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:38,000
to make them feel at home and safe and wanted.

421
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
We need to be careful not to belittle them.

422
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
We need to be careful not to, you know,

423
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
make a big thing out of their inevitable mistakes

424
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
or misconceptions about the way things go,

425
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
but just to help them along,

426
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,000
mentor and be kind and friendly to them.

427
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
The third relationship is the relationship

428
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
of the parents of new families

429
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:01,000
with the troop committee

430
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,000
and the scout master and things like that.

431
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:07,000
I have a new motto for this, called assume nothing.

432
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
Assume nothing.

433
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
I mean, if you've been around scouting for a while

434
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
and you and your family are used to it

435
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
and you know how to make things happen

436
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
and what happens at campouts and things like that,

437
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
we often will take that

438
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
and we'll kind of forget what it was like to be new

439
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:25,000
and to not know exactly what was going on with your son

440
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:26,000
or maybe where he was going

441
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
or what was going to happen at campouts and things,

442
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,000
And you know, truth be told,

443
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:31,000
I get a little tired of that.

444
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
I get a little tired of answering the same questions

445
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:34,000
over and over again,

446
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
but I do make sure that new parents

447
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
have all of my contact information

448
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
and understand that they can call me

449
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:43,000
with any question or concern that they have anytime

450
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:46,000
and we'll do our best to take care of it.

451
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
The other thing is our troop committee

452
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:53,000
puts together a little new parents orientation

453
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
and gets them together so that they can meet each other,

454
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
meet some of the other parents in the troop

455
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
and learn a little bit about the things

456
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
that are going to happen next

457
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:04,000
and how planning is done

458
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:07,000
and how preparations are made for campouts

459
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and things like that.

460
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:11,000
You know, it's a vitally important thing

461
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:14,000
that I think a lot of troops end up missing sometimes

462
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
And when parents get discouraged,

463
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:17,000
scouts get discouraged,

464
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
And when scouts get discouraged

465
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,000
they vote with their feet

466
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
and they walk away from the scouting

467
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
and they walk away from the program.

468
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
So that's just a few thoughts

469
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:28,000
on how to make new scouts welcome

470
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and how to make sure that they stay on

471
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
and become our older scouts

472
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Music.

473
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
Hi, I'm Kevin Callum, the Happy Camper

474
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:56,000
and you're listening to the Scout Master Podcast

475
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Music.

476
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,000
That's right, it's time for a scout master's minute

477
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:13,000
Music.

478
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:14,000
Well, you might not know this,

479
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:17,000
but I have spent most of my adult life as a carpenter.

480
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:20,000
That's right. I've been in the trades most of my adult life

481
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and I can tell you from experience

482
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
that one of the most essential tools

483
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,000
for a carpenter to have is an accurate measuring tape.

484
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
It's a pretty important thing.

485
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
The other thing is, I can tell you,

486
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
is that the most overrated tool

487
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that a carpenter has is a measuring tape.

488
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
And here's why

489
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
Sometimes, if you have to cut out a piece of wood

490
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
to fit into a space,

491
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:42,000
if you go and you measure

492
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:43,000
and you transfer the measurements

493
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
well, you can make a mistake

494
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
or you can misread something

495
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
A lot of times. it's easier

496
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
if it's possible to hold the piece of wood

497
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:52,000
right to the place where you need it

498
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
and mark it right. there

499
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
Don't need a measuring tape at all.

500
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
Now, what in the world does this have to do with scouts?

501
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:02,000
Well, boys are like boards.

502
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,000
I mean they're rarely uniform, featureless raw material.

503
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
They have twists and knots

504
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and difficult cross-grained areas

505
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:13,000
that has to be adapted to the task at hand.

506
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
As scout masters, we might have measuring tapes

507
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:18,000
of rules and policies and requirements

508
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,000
from advancement to work on the boys with

509
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:24,000
and to measure their performance with,

510
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:27,000
but it's rarely that accurate.

511
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:30,000
It's rarely the way that it looks

512
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:33,000
in the books and the pictures right.

513
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:37,000
What it is is. it's making do with what we have

514
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:40,000
and making sure that the measurements we use

515
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
are individual.

516
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Now your house is probably a good, secure,

517
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
dry, warm, durable place to live

518
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and probably enjoyed a great deal.

519
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:52,000
Scouts likewise. they need to be decent people,

520
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:56,000
good citizens, capable leaders, reliable,

521
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
just like a good house.

522
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:01,000
Any carpenter who insisted that every dimension

523
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:04,000
of a house be consistent to the fraction of an inch

524
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,000
would end up being really frustrated,

525
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:10,000
because the world just doesn't work that way.

526
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,000
Any scout master who wants to make similar demands

527
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
on the precision of his scouts,

528
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,000
that they all measure up to the exact same thing,

529
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
that they all look alike, that they all act alike-

530
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
is going to find that they are trading

531
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:27,000
the perfectly acceptable for the absolutely perfect.

532
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,000
A dimensionally perfect house in theory is possible,

533
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
but it wouldn't function any better

534
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
than a house that maybe was off a quarter of an inch

535
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
here and there.

536
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
And get out of measuring tape and measure your own house

537
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
if you doubt it.

538
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
There isn't one out there.

539
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
There isn't a perfectly square, perfectly level,

540
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:48,000
perfectly plumb house in existence.

541
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
It just doesn't happen.

542
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
But they're very comfortable and they're secure

543
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
and they're durable and we enjoy living in them,

544
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
just the way that we want our scouts to be.

545
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
We want them to be comfortable and secure

546
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
and durable in life

547
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
And we do that not by measuring them

548
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
to the fraction of an inch,

549
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:08,000
but by making sure that the skills

550
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
that we share with them and the maturity they reach

551
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:14,000
fits the purpose at hand.

552
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Remember, each one's a little different.

553
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
Each one measures up to the extent

554
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
of their highest reach

555
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
and his fellow scout's highest reach,

556
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
And the test of our work as Scoutmasters

557
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
is not perfect achievement,

558
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
but the character of our scout.

